package Patterns;

import java.util.Scanner;

public class Q6 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Please enter n: ");
        int n = input.nextInt();
        for (int i = 1; i <=n ; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <=n-i+1; j++) {
                System.out.print(j);

            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}

OUTPUT:
n = 5
12345
1234
123
12
1

Approach
Take an integer n as input from the user.
Use an outer loop to iterate through rows from 1 to n.
For each row i, use an inner loop to print numbers from 1 to n - i + 1.
After printing the numbers for the current row, move to the next line.
Continue until all rows are printed.

Logic
The outer loop controls the rows.
The inner loop prints numbers starting from 1.
The upper limit of the inner loop decreases by one in every row.
As a result, each row contains one fewer number than the previous row, forming an inverted number triangle.

Time Complexity

Total iterations:

n + (n-1) + (n-2) + ... + 1 = n(n + 1)/2

Time Complexity: O(n²)

Space Complexity

Space Complexity: O(1)
